// Castrol worker: unattended-upgrades must never restart the container
// runtime underneath a running cycle.
//
// Install as /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/51castrol-unattended-upgrades. It sorts after
// Ubuntu's own 50unattended-upgrades, and apt appends list entries across
// files, so this adds to the stock blacklist without editing it.
//
// Why: docker on this box is Ubuntu's `docker.io`, so its security updates
// arrive through noble-security like any other package. The package's
// postinst restarts docker.service. Both castrol units declare
// Requires=docker.service, so the restart is passed on to them as a stop.
// On 2026-11-18 that killed a 100-video batch 40 minutes in.
//
// These three are patched BY HAND, between cycles. See deploy/README.md,
// "Automatic upgrades". Everything else keeps updating on its own.
//
// Entries are regular expressions matched against the package name.

Unattended-Upgrade::Package-Blacklist {
    "docker.io$";
    "containerd$";
    "runc$";
};

// Stated, not assumed. A kernel update must never reboot the box on its own
// clock. A reboot mid-batch is survivable (OnBootSec resumes it), but it is
// a choice for a person to make between cycles.
Unattended-Upgrade::Automatic-Reboot "false";
